  "summary": "US-Mexico cooperation runs through the latest Brief. The United States said Mexico could buy drones and anti-drone systems at competitive prices, while Mexico's aviation regulator said it answered all questions in an FAA review. The US regulator has not announced a result. At home, manufacturing employment rose 0.1% in June after four monthly declines, but remained below a year earlier for a 40th month.",
  "lead": {
    "h1": "Mexico will buy US drones and anti-drone systems for security operations",
    "context": "The United States announced that Mexico will be able to buy the equipment at competitive prices.",
    "background": "Drones can extend surveillance over difficult terrain. Anti-drone systems serve a different purpose: they detect, track or disrupt unauthorized aircraft. The announcement covers both capabilities but does not identify models, quantities or delivery dates.",
    "view": "This is a procurement opening, not yet proof of a new operational capability. Its value will depend on what Mexico buys, how quickly it arrives and which agencies can use it under clear rules.",
    "prediction": "Watch for a contract or official delivery notice that names the equipment and quantity. That would show the announcement has moved from access to an actual purchase.",
